    "datePosted": "2026-11-20T08:23:21.000Z",
    "description": "Job Title: Nutrition Officer Duty Station: Afar Region – Afambo/Amibara Reports to: Project Coordinator Employment Type: Full-time No of vacant post: Two Deadline: November 24, 2026 Organizational Overview Nexus Ethiopia is a national Ethiopian Civil Society Organization committed to strengthening the resilience of vulnerable and crisis-affected communities through integrated Humanitarian–Development–Peace (HDP) Nexus programming, localization, partnership, and locally led humanitarian action. Nexus Ethiopia, in partnership with other humanitarian actors, is implementing project entitled Integrated Lifesaving Humanitarian Response for Drought, Flood and Displacement-Affected Communities in Afambo and Amibara Woredas, Afar Region. Project Background The project is a multisectoral humanitarian response designed to provide lifesaving assistance and protection to drought-, flood- and displacement-affected communities in Afar Region, Afambo and Amibara woredas. The role supports Nexus Ethiopia’s health and nutrition implementation team and works with government health services, community structures and other project sectors. Job Purpose Lead the planning, delivery and monitoring of the project’s emergency nutrition activities in Afambo and Amibara. Support facility and outreach teams to identify acute malnutrition early, link eligible clients to treatment and maintain continuity of care, working alongside the IYCF Officer. Key Responsibilities Programme planning and coordination Prepare monthly nutrition workplans and activity budgets aligned with the approved logframe and woreda priorities. Coordinate with Woreda Health Offices, health facilities and mobile health and nutrition teams; schedule outreach for underserved, displaced and pastoralist communities. Work with the IYCF Officer to link screening and treatment with caregiver counselling, and with WASH staff to address hygiene-related service gaps. Screening treatment support and referral Organize active screening of children under five and pregnant and lactating women using nationally approved tools and criteria. Support outpatient therapeutic services, clinical follow-up and treatment documentation in accordance with national protocols and the officer’s professional scope. Ensure children requiring inpatient care are promptly referred to designated stabilization services; follow up arrival and counter-referral. Coordinate tracing of missed appointments and treatment defaulters through Health Extension Workers and community structures. Monitor admission, recovery, mortality, default and non-response trends with facility teams and agree corrective actions. Quality assurance and supply management Provide supportive supervision and practical coaching to facility staff, outreach personnel and community volunteers on screening, referral and record keeping. Coordinate forecasting, stock monitoring and replenishment of therapeutic foods, nutrition equipment and related supplies with health and logistics teams. Check stock records, storage conditions and expiry risks; promptly report shortages and service interruptions. Identify staff training needs and document follow-up actions from supervision visits. Monitoring accountability and reporting Maintain verified service records and submit timely indicator updates and narrative reports to the Project Coordinator and MEAL team. Disaggregate results as required and reconcile repeat visits and referrals to avoid duplicate beneficiary reporting. Document access barriers and community feedback, follow up routine complaints and refer sensitive concerns through designated channels. Apply safeguarding, PSEA, confidentiality, disability inclusion and safe referral practices throughout implementation.",
